 2. ⚠️ Bill Gates&#x27; AI warning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Roy Rochlin/Getty Images for Netflix New overnight: Bill Gates, long a tech optimist, is sounding a sober warning about AI: It's advancing faster than governments and society are preparing for its consequences.

 In a new essay, Gates argues that "AI will either be the greatest equalizer ever invented, or the worst source of injustice." Which course it takes depends on what people do (or don't) right now, he says. 😬 "The good stuff is moving a bit slowly, and ... other than robots, I'd say the bad stuff is imminent," Gates told Axios' Ina Fried during an interview at his Seattle-area office.

 "On the current course and speed, there's a very high chance of a net negative outcome." Gates criticized both Democrats and Republicans: "I'd say neither party is very knowledgeable or being thoughtful about this." Zoom out: Gates is calling for new national institutions to coordinate AI policy across national security, employment, education, taxation and health — alongside an international body modeled partly on nuclear inspections and international aviation regulation.

 Reality check: Gates acknowledges geopolitics makes coordinated action exceptionally difficult.

 "If someone had a credible plan for slowing down AI advances globally, I would likely support it," he writes in the memo. "However, I don't think that's going to happen." Asked whether his association with Jeffrey Epstein could cause people to dismiss his arguments, Gates pushed back : "What I did do, in some ways, was very limited, and it was motivated by my desire to raise money."

 "But … it was a huge mistake. ... I made mistakes. I've changed. I'm going to be careful about stuff like that." Read Gates' essay ... Share this story . 

 
 
