 3. 💰 Rubio: Sanctions, not strikes, on Iran 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Anna Moneymaker/Getty Images Secretary of State Marco Rubio has told several foreign counterparts that "for the time being" the U.S. isn't expected to initiate new strikes against Iran, a U.S. official and a second source with knowledge of the matter tell Axios' Barak Ravid .

 Instead, he's said the focus is on other pressure, including sanctions announced this week. Why it matters: Rubio's message to allied foreign ministers comes as the U.S. believes it's suffocating Iran with the naval blockade — while moving growing amounts of oil out of the Strait of Hormuz.

 It's another sign President Trump is exasperated with the war and wants it to be over, at least for now. The U.S. official said Rubio made clear the U.S. isn't planning a return to major combat operations but didn't rule out strikes if Iran attacks first. 

 Another U.S. official said the U.S. Navy's clearing of mines from the Strait of Hormuz is a watershed moment in the war, largely neutralizing one of Iran's main sources of leverage. Share this story . 

 
 
