 5. 📝 Next billionaire flex 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios Legendary investor Stanley Druckenmiller made AI writing seem like no big deal this week, telling NOTUS reporter Jeff Stein "of course" he used it to help draft a widely shared Wall Street Journal op-ed .

 Why it matters: The moment could mark a cultural shift where AI writing is basically all good, at least among certain influential elites, Axios' Emily Peck writes. 🧑‍💻 Among his intended audience in finance, Druckenmiller's article got more attention for its substance — a scathing critique of Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent — than its style.

 The writing has the telltale cadences of the AI-generated one-two punch — for example, "If the 30-year must trade at 5.5% to clear, that isn't a crisis. It is an invoice." The big picture: The WSJ's opinion section opened the floodgates to more AI-generated op-eds with its response.

 "AI is a fact of modern life. People will use it to assist in their work and writing, including with research, checking grammar, editing and more," WSJ opinion editor Paul Gigot said in a statement. "The question for us is whether what we publish from contributors reflects an author's original argument, and if the author has the standing and credibility to make it." Worth noting: The WSJ newsroom, separate from Gigot's team, has a different AI policy . 🧮 "There's a reason I moved from an English major to being an economics major," Druckenmiller told Stein. 

 "I'm not embarrassed by it ... I write everything using AI now for the same reason I use a calculator when I do math problems." Axios' Madison Mills contributed reporting ... Share this story . 

 
 
