 6. 📚 Dolly&#x27;s literary legacy 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Dolly Parton reads to schoolchildren at the Library of Congress in 2018. Photo: Shannon Finney/Getty Images Dolly Parton, who died yesterday at 80, was a towering musician — but her proudest accomplishment was her Imagination Library . It has sent children worldwide more than 304 million free books, Axios Nashville's Adam Tamburin and Nate Rau write. 

 She founded it in 1995 to honor her father, a sharecropper who never learned to read. Before he died in 2000, Parton said , he told her the program was the most important thing she'd ever done. As she put it, "that's saying a lot." Covers of today's USA Today, New York Post 📖 "I didn't have children because I believed that God didn't mean for me to have kids, so everybody's kids could be mine so I could do things like Imagination Library," Parton told Oprah in 2020. 

 "Because if I hadn't had the freedom to work, I wouldn't have done all the things I've done." Axios Nashville's obit ... Axios Columbus' look at her literary legacy . 

