 7. 🤖 Cover du jour 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Cover photo: Jessica Chou for TIME In a TIME cover story out this morning, Alex Heath — a top tech journalist who writes the Sources newsletter about the AI race — concludes that OpenAI is "attempting two ­reinventions at once" as it prepares to go public: 

 "OpenAI now believes it has fixed the product and operational failures that allowed Anthropic to seize pole position in the AI race." "At the same time, it is using the worst safety crisis in its history to make a bid for the safety-minded identity its main rival [Anthropic] has long claimed: the frontier lab willing to slow down when the technology becomes too dangerous." In late July, after the Hugging Face breach , OpenAI's research team "froze some experiments and slowed other work while it tightened its sandboxes and expanded monitoring." 🦾 In Heath's 4,300-word cover story , based on dozens of hours of interviews, OpenAI chief research officer Mark Chen estimates the company is "80% of the way" to AGI. 

 OpenAI president and co-founder Greg Brockman tells TIME that, viewed from two years in the future, this may be remembered as the moment AGI was created. Altman says OpenAI is "not quite yet" there, but that by the end of the year the company will have an internal system he would call AGI. Altman tells Heath: "We clearly had some missteps as a company ... Both in terms of product direction and specifically on pretraining in research, we fell behind where we wanted to be."

